course: biology
lesson: 165
title: The Australian outback: red kangaroos, emus, thorny devils and runaway camels and toads
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The outback is the huge dry middle of Australia: red sand, low bushes, rare rain and heat that can bake the ground, yet it is full of animals that have found clever ways to live there.
explain: A red kangaroo bounds along like a pogo stick, and its springy tendons bounce it forward so it hardly gets tired, while a thorny devil drinks by standing in damp sand and letting tiny grooves suck the water up to its mouth like a straw.
explain: People also brought animals that ran wild: dingoes thousands of years ago, then camels and cane toads, and the toads have become a poisonous pest.
explain: After rare heavy rain the desert can burst into flowers within weeks, and frogs that waited underground for years climb out to breed.
fact: The red kangaroo is the largest marsupial and can hop at over fifty kilometres an hour; males wrestle and kick each other with their strong back legs.
fact: Kangaroos cannot easily move backwards, and people like to say this is why the kangaroo and the emu, which also rarely steps back, are on the Australian coat of arms.
fact: Emus are the second largest birds in the world after the ostrich and can run very fast.
fact: In 1932 Australian soldiers with machine guns tried to cull (kill off) emus eating farm crops in Western Australia and largely failed; it is remembered as the Emu War.
fact: The thorny devil has tiny grooves between its spines that pull water from damp sand up to its mouth, and it has a false head on its neck that it shows when attacked.
fact: The frilled lizard spreads a frill around its neck, held open by rods of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ears, like an umbrella, to look bigger and frightening.
fact: Some termites in northern Australia build tall flat mounds, called magnetic termite mounds, lined up north to south, which helps keep the inside cooler than the outside.
fact: Dingoes are wild dogs that came to Australia with people several thousand years ago, and a dingo fence over five thousand kilometres long protects sheep farms.
fact: Many desert animals, like hopping mice, get almost all their water from their food, and waterholes fill with animals in the cool of morning and evening.
fact: The bilby is a desert marsupial with long rabbit-like ears that digs spiral burrows, and Australians buy chocolate bilbies at Easter to raise money to save them.
fact: Australia has the biggest herd of wild camels in the world, descended from camels brought in the 1800s to carry goods across the desert.
fact: Cane toads were brought to Australia in 1935 to eat beetles in sugar cane, but they spread across much of northern Australia, and animals like quolls die when they eat these poisonous toads.
fact: After rare rains the outback can burst into flowers, and some frogs dig into the ground and wait years for the next rain.
exercise: Which is the largest marsupial? | The red kangaroo | The koala | The wombat | The bilby
exercise: How fast can a red kangaroo hop? | Over fifty kilometres an hour | Only about five kilometres an hour | Over two hundred kilometres an hour | Only about one kilometre an hour
exercise: Why do people say the kangaroo and the emu are on Australia's coat of arms? | Neither easily moves backwards, so they stand for going forward | They are the only animals that live anywhere in Australia, so there was no other choice | They were the first king and queen | They are the most dangerous animals
exercise: Which is the only bird bigger than the emu? | The ostrich | The penguin | The eagle | The swan
exercise: What happened in the Emu War of 1932? | Soldiers with machine guns tried to cull emus and largely failed | Emus attacked a city | Emus were trained as soldiers | Soldiers won easily in one day and every emu in the wheat fields was gone
exercise: How does a thorny devil drink from damp sand? | Tiny grooves between its spines pull water up to its mouth | It licks the wet sand at night with a long sticky tongue | It digs a well with its claws | It never needs any water
exercise: What does a thorny devil do when attacked? | Dips its real head and shows a false head on its neck | Squirts blood from its eyes | Jumps into a tree | Changes colour to bright blue so that birds think it is poisonous
exercise: Why does a frilled lizard open its frill? | To look bigger and frightening | To catch rain to drink | To fly from rock to rock | To cool its eggs
exercise: How are magnetic termite mounds lined up? | North to south | From east to west | In a perfect circle | In no pattern at all
exercise: How did dingoes get to Australia? | They came with people several thousand years ago | They walked from Africa on land | They were always there, living alongside the dinosaurs | They came on ships in 1990
exercise: What is the dingo fence for? | Keeping dingoes away from sheep farms | Keeping kangaroos in the outback so they do not hop into the cities | Keeping camels in a zoo | Stopping the sand from blowing
exercise: Where do hopping mice get most of their water? | From their food | From the salty sea | From waterholes every hour | From licking rocks at noon
exercise: When do animals gather at outback waterholes? | In the cool of morning and evening | Only at midday, when the heat is at its worst | Only on rainy days | Never, they avoid water
exercise: Why do Australians buy chocolate bilbies at Easter? | To raise money to save bilbies | Because rabbits are banned | Because bilbies lay eggs | Because bilbies love chocolate
exercise: Why does Australia have so many wild camels? | Camels brought to carry goods in the 1800s were let go and spread | Camels have always lived there | They swam from Arabia | Zoos let a few of them out last year and they spread very fast
exercise: Why were cane toads brought to Australia in 1935? | To eat beetles in sugar cane | To be eaten as food by the farmers | As friendly pets for children in the towns | To clean ponds of mosquito larvae
exercise: Why do quolls often die near cane toads? | They eat the poisonous toads | The toads hunt them down and eat them | The toads drink all the water | The toads steal their burrows
exercise: What can happen in the outback after rare heavy rain? | The desert bursts into flowers and frogs come out to breed | The sand turns into ice | All the animals leave the desert and go to live by the sea | Nothing at all changes
test: «2 kangaroos and an emu at the waterhole, how many legs, arms don't count» | 6 | 8 | 10 | 4
test: «my uncle says emus won the war against the army lmao, made that up right» What is right? | It really happened: in 1932 soldiers largely failed to cull emus | It is a made-up joke | Emus were in the army | It was a war against kangaroos that ate all the wheat on the farms
test: In a cartoon a kangaroo is a boxing champion who punches with gloves. What is true of real red kangaroos? | Males do wrestle and kick with strong back legs, but no gloves or rings | They wear gloves in the wild | They never fight at all | They punch only with their tails, which is why the tails are so thick and strong
test: «why are there camels in australia, isn't that like arabia» What is right? | People brought them in the 1800s and many ran wild | They walked across from Arabia over a land bridge long ago | They are actually large kangaroos | There are no camels in Australia
test: What would happen to the inside of a magnetic termite mound if it faced the midday sun with a broad side? | It would probably get hotter | It would freeze over at night and stay cold | Nothing could change, it is always the same | It would fill with water and become a lake
test: «gonna bring a cane toad home as a pet for my dog to play with» What is right? | No, cane toads are poisonous and a dog could die from biting one | Yes, they are safe toys | Yes, dogs love to eat them | No, they bite people's fingers off with their sharp teeth, so keep away
test: A lizard with spines stands still in wet sand and its back looks damp. What is it doing? | Drinking through grooves that pull water to its mouth | Sunbathing | Laying eggs | Washing itself
test: «4 bilbies, how many big ears, quick» | 8 | 4 | 16 | 2
test: Why might a desert frog wait underground for years? | To survive dry years until rain makes pools to breed in | Because it is hibernating from the snow, like a bear in winter | Because it is scared of kangaroos | Because it cannot find the way up
test: A dingo, a sheep farmer and a long fence. What is the fence for? | Keeping dingoes away from the farmer's sheep | Keeping sheep away from dingoes' puppies | Keeping the hot sun off the sheep by holding up a shade cloth | Stopping emus from flying
test: «a lizard just puffed out a giant collar at me n hissed, is it gonna eat me» What is right? | No, the frilled lizard is trying to look scary so you leave it alone | Yes, it eats people | Yes, it is about to spit fire at you from its open mouth | No, it wants a hug
exam: Identify the principal means by which the thorny devil obtains water within an arid environment. | Fine grooves between its spines conduct moisture from damp sand to its mouth | It excavates deep wells in the sand with its strong foreclaws during the night | It consumes the dew of flowers exclusively | It dispenses with water entirely throughout its life
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the introduction of the cane toad into Australia. | It was introduced in 1935 for the control of beetles in sugar cane and subsequently became a harmful pest | It was introduced as a source of cheap food for the population of the outback towns during the war | It arrived spontaneously by swimming from Asia | It has always been native to the continent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the red kangaroo? | It is the largest of the marsupials and is capable of hopping at speeds exceeding fifty kilometres per hour | It is the smallest of the marsupials | It is a placental mammal related to the deer, and it moves about by leaping from rock to rock | It moves exclusively by walking on all four limbs
exam: Ascertain the origin of the dingo upon the Australian continent. | It arrived in the company of people several thousand years ago | It descended from the marsupial wolves of the dinosaur age | It was introduced by sheep farmers in the twentieth century | It evolved from the kangaroo
